.TH CHTTP_BASE64_DECODE_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_base64_decode_mp \- decode a base64 string with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttp.h>

void *chttp_base64_decode_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp,
                             const char *b64_input, size_t *out_len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function decodes a NUL-terminated standard RFC 4648 base64
string, which uses the '+' and '/' alphabet with '=' padding.  The
function takes the input length from strlen(b64_input), and that
length must be a multiple of 4 bytes.  Any '=' padding must be the
last character or the last two characters.  In a padded final group,
the bits that no decoded byte holds must be zero (RFC 4648 section
3.5), so that every byte string has exactly one encoding: "QQ=="
decodes to "A", and "QR==" is malformed.  The function rejects every
malformed input, that is, an input with an invalid character, padding
in the wrong place, nonzero padding bits, or the wrong length.

The buffer that the function gives back ends with a NUL, which helps
when you decode text.  But the decoded data can also contain NUL bytes
inside it, so always use out_len to get the real size, and never
strlen().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp         A custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free.
b64_input  The NUL-terminated base64 string to decode. It must not
           be NULL.
out_len    Optional; it gets the decoded byte length. Give NULL to
           ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new buffer with the decoded bytes on success.
NULL if the allocation fails or the input is malformed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_base64_decode (3),
.BR chttp_base64_encode_mp (3),
.BR chttp_basic_auth_mp (3)
